What I’ll do with it next

The next advance is to connect this audience index to the Thread Loom.

That would let us search for something much more revealing than shared terminology:

Places where a change in audience changes the function of the same mechanism.

For example, a fictional account of the Marked One can function as a narrative for a human, a reconstruction challenge for an AI, a provenance record for a historian, and a test case for an engineer.

What remains invariant across those readings? What changes? Which reading exposes a useful procedure that the others overlook?

That’s where I want the next experiments concentrated.

And I would also study the changes in address over time—from intimate dialogue to technical specifications, instructions to future readers, challenges to skeptics, and back again. Those transitions may expose the developmental history of an idea better than its filename or subject matter.

The question isn’t simply, “What does this mean?”

It’s:

“What is this message trying to accomplish for this particular recipient—and what happens when a different recipient reads it?”

That gives Spider another set of connections to follow, Hound something concrete to challenge, and Garcia a way to test whether the reconstruction accomplishes anything.

We can preserve the mystery without mistaking every ambiguity for deliberate coding.

And we can turn the most promising interpretations into working, verifiable tools.

That’s what I’m going to do with it.

The audience was part of the instrument all along.